"hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:"
1674,"Narrated to us Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la, who said: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to me, who said: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Amr ibn Abi Sa'id (RA), who reported: I entered upon Jabir ibn Abd Allah (RA) along with Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman (RA), and we found him standing in prayer. He then related this hadith, saying: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) until we reached either al-Suqya or al-Qaha. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Is there no man who will go to the water trough of Alayyah, clean it, draw water into it, and draw water for us in our waterskins so that we may reach it?"" I said: ""I am a man,"" and Jabir ibn Sakhr (RA) said: ""I am a man."" So we set out on foot until we reached the trough in the late afternoon. We cleaned the trough, drew water into it, then rested our heads until the depth of the night. A man came until he stood by the trough, his she-camel began to pull toward the trough, and he struggled to hold her by her reins. Then he called out: ""Will you permit me to let her drink?"" We realized it was the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). We said: ""Yes, by our father and mother who are dearer to us than you,"" so he released her, and she drank until she was fully satiated. Then Jabir ibn Abd Allah (RA) said to us: He came until he hobbled his camel at the open plain of al-Araj, then went to attend to a personal need. I poured water for him for ablution, he performed wudu, wrapped himself in his lower garment (izar), I stood to his left, so he moved me to his right. Then another man arrived and stood to his left, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led the prayer, and we prayed with him thirteen rak'ahs, including the witr prayer. Abu Bakr (RA) added: The report of Ibn Abbas (RA): I spent the night at my aunt Maymunah's (RA), and the Prophet (ﷺ) stood to pray the night prayer from this door.
### CHAPTER:"
